## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical E2E skills: gating UX with feature flags, deterministic tests, data seeding, and test isolation using Cypress or Playwright.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and gating
- Test data seeding per run
- LocalStorage state as idempotence marker
- Network/mocks to deterministically simulate flag responses
- Time-bounded execution for beginner scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright skeleton
test('onboarding respects flag', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/flags**', r => r.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ onboarding: true }) }));
  await page.goto('https://app.local/');
  await expect(page.locator('#onboard-modal')).toBeVisible();
  await page.locator('#finish').click();
  const stored = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('onboarding_complete'));
  expect(stored).toBe('true');
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#onboard-modal')).toBeHidden();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to multi-tenant setups with per-tenant flag overrides?
- How would you ensure the test remains reliable when the flag toggles during a session?

## Why This Is Asked
Testing offline-first sync across devices is a realistic challenge for consumer apps with data integrity concerns, multi-device usage, and eventual consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first architecture: Data is persisted locally and synced when connectivity is restored
- Conflict resolution strategy: Merge (combine changes) vs. Last-Write-Wins (most recent timestamp wins)
- Test isolation across devices: Each Playwright context maintains separate local storage and cookies
- Deterministic network shaping: Use Playwright's `route` or Cypress's `server` to intercept and delay requests

## Code Example
```javascript
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('offline-first sync with conflict resolution', async ({ browser }) => {
  // Create two isolated device contexts
  const ctxA = await browser.newContext();
  const ctxB = await browser.newContext();

  const pageA = await ctxA.newPage();
  const pageB = await ctxB.newPage();

  // Simulate offline on both devices
  await pageA.route('**/api/**', route => route.abort('failed'));
  await pageB.route('**/api/**', route => route.abort('failed'));

  // Device A creates a note offline
  await pageA.goto('/app');
  await pageA.fill('[data-testid="note-input"]', 'Note from A');
  await pageA.click('[data-testid="save"]');

  // Device B creates a note offline
  await pageB.goto('/app');
  await pageB.fill('[data-testid="note-input"]', 'Note from B');
  await pageB.click('[data-testid="save"]');

  // Restore connectivity
  await pageA.unrouteAll();
  await pageB.unrouteAll();

  // Wait for sync and verify
  await pageA.reload();
  await pageB.reload();

  await expect(pageA.locator('[data-testid="notes"]')).toContainText('Note from A');
  await expect(pageA.locator('[data-testid="notes"]')).toContainText('Note from B');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 5+ devices?
- How would you verify conflict resolution for concurrent edits to the same note?
- How would you test partial sync failures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests around feature flags in E2E flows are common in production environments. This evaluates the ability to design deterministic tests for both flag states, validate calculations, and ensure order completion under different configurations.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags and gated flows
- API-driven data seeding for test setup
- Network and service mocking for external dependencies such as tax and fraud services
- Deterministic assertions and test isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test skeleton
async function testFlag(flagValue) {
  // Toggle feature flag via API
  await api.post('/api/feature-flags', { flag: 'new-checkout', enabled: flagValue });

  // Seed cart and user
  await api.post('/api/test/seed', { userId: 'user-123', items: [...] });

  // Mock external services
  await page.route('**/api/tax*', route => route.fulfill({ body: { rate: flagValue ? 0.085 : 0 } }));
  await page.route('**/api/fraud*', route => route.fulfill({ body: { status: 'pass' } }));

  // Navigate to checkout and complete flow
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await page.click('#place-order');

  // Assert final total and order completion
  const finalTotal = await page.textContent('#total');
  expect(finalTotal).toBe(flagValue ? '$108.50' : '$100.00');
  expect(await page.locator('.order-success')).toBeVisible();
}

// Run tests for both flag states
test('Legacy checkout', () => testFlag(false));
test('New checkout', () => testFlag(true));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle rate limiting on the feature flag API?
- What strategies would you use to debug flaky tests in the fraud check flow?
- How do you ensure test data isolation between parallel test runs?

